      "writtenOn": "2026-03-25T19:18:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e VK_DYNAMIC_STATE_CULL_MODE specifies that the cullMode state in VkPipelineRasterizationStateCreateInfo will be ignored and must be set dynamically with vkCmdSetCullMode before any drawing commands.\n\nThis is now ignored by Vulkan so IIUC it\u0027s only used when generating the cache key. This still seems problematic to me? Here is an example where we have pipeline that only vary in cullMode: \n\n1. First pipeline is created that uses VK_CULL_MODE_FRONT_BIT. It gets turned into dynamic state and rasterization.cullMode is set to VK_CULL_MODE_NONE before generating the cache key.\n2. Second almost identical pipeline is created that uses VK_CULL_MODE_BACK_BIT. The cached entry has VK_CULL_MODE_NONE so cache lookup fails and a new pipeline is created. After the new pipeline is created the cache key will be the same as pipeline #1 which is a collision.\n3. A third pipeline identical to the first is created that uses VK_CULL_MODE_FRONT_BIT. The cached entry has VK_CULL_MODE_NONE so we don\u0027t find the cached entry and create a new pipeline again.\n4. A fourth almost identical pipeline is created with VK_CULL_MODE_NONE. This matches the cached entry, which dynamic sets the state to VK_CULL_MODE_FRONT_BIT, and produces the wrong results.",
      "revId": "b9709a0ef01574ab7a517d9c9d389de80d8fbfc4",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "41d7ad2b_1f7b90ec",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/vulkan/RenderPipelineVk.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 576,
      "author": {
        "id": 1001591
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-03-25T23:10:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I worried about this initially as well. After some digging I found that the cache key also takes into account the dynamic state array that is set as part of the VkPipelineDynamicStateCreateInfo, so a pipeline with VK_DYNAMIC_STATE_CULL_MODE_EXT and VK_CULL_MODE_NONE will be considered distinct from a pipeline with only VK_CULL_MODE_NONE. (Also, we shouldn\u0027t be in a state where some pipelines use dynamic state and some don\u0027t, but even if we are that should be handled correctly here.)",
      "parentUuid": "580517d8_47fdb720",
      "revId": "b9709a0ef01574ab7a517d9c9d389de80d8fbfc4",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f77e938f_1f0a3ca9",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/vulkan/RenderPipelineVk.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 576,
      "author": {
        "id": 1004831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-03-26T13:14:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e After some digging I found that the cache key also takes into account the dynamic state array that is set as part of the VkPipelineDynamicStateCreateInfo, so a pipeline with VK_DYNAMIC_STATE_CULL_MODE_EXT and VK_CULL_MODE_NONE\n\nHow does that work exactly? `VkPipelineDynamicStateCreateInfo::pDynamicStates` only has enum `VK_DYNAMIC_STATE_CULL_MODE_EXT` to identify which piece of state is dynamic and not the state value itself. \n\nI guess more generally this CL could use a test to show that creating a second pipeline which varies only in dynamic state is a cache miss that creates a unique RenderPipeline object and creating a second pipeline that is identical to the first is still a cache hit.",
      "parentUuid": "41d7ad2b_1f7b90ec",
      "revId": "b9709a0ef01574ab7a517d9c9d389de80d8fbfc4",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7f8bb48f_49098592",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/vulkan/RenderPipelineVk.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 576,
      "author": {
        "id": 1001591
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-03T00:01:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The goal of this CL isn\u0027t specifically to produce more cache hits, but to establish the dynamic state handling. Any cache benefits are a happy side effect.\n\nDiscussed the cache behavior with Loko today, who did the initial Vulkan blob cache work. Here\u0027s the conclusions we came to:\n\nAs is, this code should get the same PipelineCache object for pipelines that vary only by the listed dynamic states. The Vulkan blob cache can then restore the pipeline from the disk cache if a compatible pipeline has already been compiled.\n\nThat will almost certainly result in a unique VkPipeline object, however, and whether or not the Vulkan driver is able to detect that the pipelines are the same and thus skip any state setting when they are bound in sequence is driver specific. We hope they will but have no guarantees.\n\nWe could use this code to do a bit of awkward pipeline cache hit huristics as long as we\u0027re not using the monolithic cash, and as long as we clear all the disk caches before running. At that point we could infer that anything that queries the same PipelineCache object is compatible. That\u0027s pretty clunky though.\n\nSo the plan is this: We can probably move this forward as-is, though we don\u0027t want to enable it by default yet. The current code _may_ offer some benefits reducing compile times of compatible pipelines, but if we want guarantees about reducing redundant state-setting and limiting the number of VkPipeline objects created then we\u0027ll want an intermediate cache. That will happen in a follow up. If we want to rig something up to see how many pipelines this is potentially saving us we could probably do so once this lands, regardless of performance benefits, it\u0027ll just be a more manual process than is ideal.",
      "parentUuid": "f77e938f_1f0a3ca9",
      "revId": "b9709a0ef01574ab7a517d9c9d389de80d8fbfc4",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
